issue(id="0011" status="open")
  title Stress test minimum whitespace restoration
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of puggers
    i-want a focused stress suite for restoring the minimum necessary whitespace
    so-that formatter output stays readable without inventing spaces that change meaning
  premise.
    The inverse problem to compaction is restoration: when the formatter has
    normalized structure or reflowed content, what is the minimum whitespace it
    must reintroduce so tags, text, and prose still read correctly? Pug makes
    this tricky because sometimes touching is correct, sometimes one space is
    required, and sometimes preserved authored spacing is the only safe choice.
    A dedicated restoration stress suite should validate the smallest safe
    whitespace footprint across examples where missing spaces, extra spaces, or
    misplaced line breaks visibly alter output.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Add fixtures that exercise minimum-whitespace restoration for adjacent tags and text, punctuation boundaries, piped lines, interpolation, and prose-like dotted blocks.
    criterion Define expected output for cases where restored whitespace must be zero, one space, one blank line, or exact authored spacing.
    criterion Include adversarial examples where a naive “always add one space” rule would be wrong.
    criterion Produce guidance that later text-formatting and line-width tasks can use when deciding whether and how to restore spacing.
  review-items
    item(status="open") Decide whether minimum whitespace restoration should be evaluated from rendered HTML expectations, source readability expectations, or both.
    item(status="open") Confirm whether restoration policy differs between inline text and block-oriented prose contexts.
    item(status="open") Keep examples from the whitespace-control docs prominent because they capture real user expectations better than abstract synthetic cases alone.
